ng $1$ to both sides of the above inequality gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡x+1\\le 1+1 \\\\ sin⁡x+1\\le 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $sin⁡x+1$ is $A=2.$" 13194139533679 true] [17592186246278 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246279 13194139533679 true] [17592186246278 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533679 true] [17592186246278 1041 "field-1" 13194139533679 true] [17592186246278 1104 #edb/ref 17592186246279 13194139533679 true] [17592186246278 1110 #uuid "02f6e93b-5a17-558b-a6b5-7460fd4eb482" 13194139533679 true] [17592186246279 1034 "2" 13194139533679 true] [17592186246279 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533679 true] [17592186246279 1111 #uuid "e5feb501-f60a-5019-9e82-340fe0d6a961" 13194139533679 true] [17592186246280 1017 #edb/ref 17592186246281 13194139533679 true] [17592186246280 1089 "q-167633" 13194139533679 true] [17592186246280 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533679 true] [17592186246280 1100 "Consider the functions $f(x)={x}^{B}+Ax,g(x)=B{e}^{x},$ and $h(x)=sin⁡x+1,$ where $A$ and $B$ are constants to be found.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nComplete the following facts about the function $y=sin⁡x.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The domain of the function is $x\\in ($\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\infty$                \n                                    \n                            